# Synopsis

List files and directories under a directory that match one or more
glob patterns. Reading is read-only and needs no approval.

# Patterns

"patterns" is a glob pattern or a list of glob patterns; a path matches
when it matches any of them. A single pattern is a string; several
patterns are a JSON array of strings (not a JSON-encoded string), for
example {"patterns": ["*.txt", "*.md"]}.

The glob semantics are those of POSIX pathname expansion, plus the
recursive "**" extension: "**" matches zero or more path components, so
"**/name" matches "name" at any depth, and "dir/**" matches "dir" itself
and everything below it. Patterns are relative to "path" (the working
directory by default).

Dot-prefixed (hidden) names are matched only by a pattern component that
starts with "."; other components skip them. For example ".*" lists the
hidden entries directly under "path", and "foo/.*" those directly under
"foo".

- "*.txt"     files ending in ".txt" directly under "path"
- "**/*.txt"  files ending in ".txt" at any depth under "path"
- "sub/*"     entries directly under "sub"
- "sub"       the entry "sub" itself
- "foo/.*"    hidden entries directly under "foo"

# Result

The result lists the matched paths. The first line reports the number
of paths shown and the total number of matches:

    <count> of <total> listed:
    <path>
    <path>
    ...

The paths are absolute, sorted, and deduplicated. A directory path ends
with "/". A path containing control characters is shown shell-quoted in
the `$'...'` (dollar single quote) form. "limit" (default 100) caps the
number of paths shown: "total" is the number of all matches before the
cap, and "count" is the number actually shown.

# JSON format

Set the "output_json" argument to true to return a JSON object instead:

    {"total": <int>, "count": <int>, "paths": [<string>, ...]}

"total" is the number of all matches, "count" the number of paths shown
(after "limit"), and "paths" the shown paths (absolute, sorted,
deduplicated, directories ending with "/").

A "bad argument" error (the argument is not valid JSON) is always
reported in the text format, because "output_json" cannot be read from
a malformed argument.

# Notes

- "limit" must be non-negative.
- A pattern matches directly under "path" unless it contains "**" to
  match across directory levels.
